/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/pcep.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("pcep", [["pc1",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(7 // 2, 7 % 2, -7 // 2)",["3 1 -3","3.5 1 -3.5","3 -1 -4","3 1 -4"],3,"// is floor division, so 7 // 2 is 3 and -7 // 2 rounds down to -4, not toward zero (-3). 7 % 2 is 1. Neither operator returns a float for int operands.","Objective 1.4 Operators",["This treats -7 // 2 as truncation toward zero; floor division rounds down, so the result is -4, not -3.","These are true-division results; // is floor division and returns an int (3 and -4) for int operands.","7 % 2 is 1, not -1; the remainder takes the sign of the divisor, which is positive here.",null],3],["pc2",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(2 ** 3 ** 2)",["512","64","36","12"],0,"The ** operator is right-associative, so 2 ** 3 ** 2 is 2 ** (3 ** 2) = 2 ** 9 = 512. Reading it left to right gives the tempting 64.","Objective 1.4 Operators",[null,"64 is (2 ** 3) ** 2, which reads ** left to right; ** is right-associative, so the exponent 3 ** 2 is evaluated first.","36 would be (2 * 3) ** 2 or 6 squared; nothing in the expression multiplies 2 by 3.","12 comes from treating ** like multiplication (2 * 3 * 2); ** is exponentiation."],2],["pc3",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(0o17 + 0x10 + 0b11)",["44","40","34","28"],2,"0o17 is octal 15, 0x10 is hex 16 and 0b11 is binary 3, so the sum is 34. Literals in different bases are all just ints and add normally.","Objective 1.3 Literals",["44 reads 0o17 as decimal 17 and 0b11 as decimal 11; they are octal 15 and binary 3.","40 misreads one of the literals; 0o17 is 15, 0x10 is 16 and 0b11 is 3, which add to 34, not 40.",null,"28 would require 0x10 to be 10; hexadecimal 10 is 16, not decimal 10."],2],["pc4",0,1,"A student expects this code to print 13. What does it actually print?\nx = 5\nx *= 2 + 3\nprint(x)",["13","25","10","28"],1,"A compound assignment evaluates the whole right side first: x *= 2 + 3 means x = x * (2 + 3) = 25. The 13 answer wrongly applies x * 2 before adding 3.","Objective 1.4 Operators",["13 applies x * 2 first and then adds 3; a compound assignment evaluates the whole right-hand side (2 + 3) before multiplying.",null,"10 ignores the + 3 entirely and computes only x * 2.","28 does not correspond to any grouping of 5, 2 and 3 here; the right side is 5, so x becomes 5 * 5."],3],["pc5",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(1 / 2 * 4)",["2","0.125","0","2.0"],3,"/ always returns a float, and * and / have equal priority evaluated left to right: (1 / 2) * 4 = 2.0. The int 2 would need //.","Objective 1.4 Operators",["The int 2 would need floor division; / always returns a float, so the result prints as 2.0.","0.125 comes from computing 1 / (2 * 4); * and / have equal priority and are evaluated left to right, so it is (1 / 2) * 4.","0 treats / as integer division as in some other languages; in Python 3, 1 / 2 is 0.5, not 0.",null],2],["pc6",0,1,"A script prints a date-like string. What is the exact output?\nprint(1, 2, 3, sep=\"-\", end=\"!\")",["1 2 3!","1-2-3-!","-1-2-3!","1-2-3!"],3,"sep is placed only between arguments and end replaces the final newline, so the output is 1-2-3!. sep is not added after the last argument.","Objective 1.5 Console I/O",["This output uses the default space separator; sep=\"-\" replaces the spaces between arguments with hyphens.","sep is placed only between arguments, not after the last one, so there is no hyphen before the !.","sep is never printed before the first argument; it goes only between values.",null],1],["pc7",0,1,"A user types 5 and presses Enter when this program runs. What is printed?\nx = input()\nprint(x * 2)",["10","55","5 5","A TypeError"],1,"input() always returns a string, so x is '5' and '5' * 2 repeats it to '55'. To get 10 the value must first be converted with int().","Objective 1.5 Console I/O",["10 would need the input converted with int(); input() returns the string '5', and '5' * 2 repeats it.",null,"String repetition joins copies with no separator; print would only add a space between separate arguments.","Multiplying a string by an int is valid (repetition); a TypeError would come from str + int, not str * int."],2],["pc8",0,1,"A developer is naming variables for a new script. Which name is not a legal Python identifier?",["_total2","total_2nd","Total","2nd_total"],3,"Identifiers cannot start with a digit, so 2nd_total is a syntax error. Leading underscores, digits after the first character and capital letters are all legal.","Objective 1.3 Variables",["A leading underscore is allowed in identifiers; _total2 is legal (it is conventionally a private name).","Digits are allowed anywhere except the first character, so total_2nd is legal.","Identifiers are case-sensitive and may start with a capital letter; Total is legal, just unusual for a variable under PEP 8.",null],1],["pc9",0,1,"Which of these words is a reserved Python keyword that cannot be used as a variable name?",["print","input","null","None"],3,"None is a keyword. print and input are built-in functions (they can be shadowed, though you should not), and null has no meaning in Python.","Objective 1.2 Keywords",["print is a built-in function, not a keyword; it can be shadowed by assignment, although you should not do it.","input is a built-in function name, not a reserved keyword, so assigning to it is legal (but bad practice).","null is not a Python keyword or built-in at all; it is an ordinary legal identifier. Python uses None instead.",null],1],["pc10",0,1,"Which statement correctly describes interpreting compared with compiling, as the terms are used in PCEP?",["An interpreter translates and runs source code each time it is run","An interpreter produces a stand-alone machine-code file once","A compiler checks only indentation and leaves syntax to run time","Interpreted languages cannot report syntax errors at all"],0,"An interpreter reads and executes the source each run, while a compiler translates the whole program once into a machine-code file that runs without the source. The stand-alone executable describes compilation, not interpretation.","Objective 1.1 Fundamental terms",[null,"Producing a stand-alone machine-code file once describes compilation, not interpretation.","Compilers check the full syntax of the program, not just indentation; this description matches neither approach.","Python is interpreted and still reports syntax errors before running any line of the file."],1],["pc11",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(bool(0.0), bool(\"0\"))",["False False","False True","True True","True False"],1,"Zero values and empty strings are False; any non-empty string is True, even '0'. So bool(0.0) is False and bool('0') is True.","Objective 1.4 Type casting",["bool(\"0\") is True because any non-empty string is truthy; the string's content is not examined.",null,"bool(0.0) is False because zero of any numeric type is falsy.","This swaps both results: 0.0 is falsy and the non-empty string \"0\" is truthy."],1],["pc12",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(int(\"12\") + float(\"3.5\"))",["123.5","15.5","15","A TypeError"],1,"int('12') is 12 and float('3.5') is 3.5; adding an int and a float gives the float 15.5. The strings are converted before the + runs, so there is no concatenation.","Objective 1.4 Type casting",["123.5 would be string concatenation; the strings are converted to numbers first, so + adds 12 and 3.5.",null,"Adding an int to a float produces a float, so the result is 15.5, not a truncated 15.","There is no TypeError because both operands are already numbers after int() and float() convert them."],2],["pc13",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(6 & 3, 6 | 3, 6 ^ 3)",["1 7 5","2 5 7","2 7 5","3 7 5"],2,"6 is 110 and 3 is 011 in binary. AND gives 010 (2), OR gives 111 (7) and XOR gives 101 (5). XOR keeps only bits set in exactly one operand.","Objective 1.4 Bitwise operators",["6 & 3 is 2 (110 & 011 = 010), not 1; the other two values are right.","This swaps the OR and XOR results; 6 | 3 is 7 and 6 ^ 3 is 5.",null,"6 & 3 is 2; AND keeps only bits set in both operands, and only the middle bit is shared."],2],["pc14",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(~5, 1 << 3)",["-5 8","4 8","-6 6","-6 8"],3,"~x equals -x - 1, so ~5 is -6. 1 << 3 shifts left three bits, multiplying by 2 ** 3 = 8. ~ is not simple negation, which is why -5 is wrong.","Objective 1.4 Bitwise operators",["~ is bitwise NOT, which gives -x - 1, so ~5 is -6, not simple negation to -5.","4 is not ~5; bitwise NOT on Python ints gives -x - 1, which is -6.","1 << 3 multiplies 1 by 2 ** 3 = 8; 6 would be adding 3 twice or a different operation.",null],1],["pc15",0,1,"A billing script compares money amounts stored as floats. What does this line print?\nprint(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)",["False","True","0.3","A ValueError"],0,"0.1 and 0.2 cannot be stored exactly in binary floating point, so their sum is 0.30000000000000004 and the comparison is False. Floats have limited accuracy.","Objective 1.4 Float accuracy",[null,"The comparison would be True only with exact decimal arithmetic; binary floats make 0.1 + 0.2 equal 0.30000000000000004.","== returns a Boolean, not a number, so the line can only print True or False.","Comparing floats never raises ValueError; the expression simply evaluates to False."],2],["pc16",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(10 - 2 * 3 ** 2)",["-8","72","-26","64"],0,"** binds tightest, then *, then -: 3 ** 2 = 9, 2 * 9 = 18, 10 - 18 = -8. 72 comes from wrongly doing 10 - 2 first.","Objective 1.4 Operator priority",[null,"72 comes from computing (10 - 2) * 3 ** 2; subtraction has lower priority than * and **.","-26 does not match any valid grouping; the correct steps are 3 ** 2 = 9, 2 * 9 = 18, 10 - 18.","64 comes from squaring (10 - 2); ** binds only to its immediate operands, 3 ** 2, and * is applied before -."],2],["pc17",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(1e3)",["1000","1e3","0.001","1000.0"],3,"A literal in scientific notation such as 1e3 is always a float, so it prints 1000.0. It is 1 times 10 to the power 3, not 10 to the power -3.","Objective 1.3 Literals",["Scientific notation always creates a float, so the value prints as 1000.0, not the int 1000.","print shows the value, not the source literal; 1e3 is displayed in normal float form as 1000.0.","0.001 would be 1e-3; the positive exponent in 1e3 means 1 times 10 to the power 3.",null],1],["pc18",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = 7\nif x > 5:\n    print(\"A\")\nelif x > 3:\n    print(\"B\")\nelse:\n    print(\"C\")",["B","C","A","A, then B on the next line"],2,"Only the first true branch of an if-elif-else chain runs. x > 5 is true, so A prints and the elif is skipped even though x > 3 is also true.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements",["x > 3 is also true, but the elif is only tested when the if condition is false; x > 5 already matched.","else runs only when every earlier condition is false, and x > 5 is true for 7.",null,"An if-elif-else chain runs at most one branch, so B is never printed after A."],2],["pc19",0,2,"What does this loop print?\nfor i in range(2, 10, 3):\n    print(i, end=\" \")",["2 5 8 11","2 5 8","3 6 9","2 4 6 8"],1,"range(2, 10, 3) starts at 2 and steps by 3 while staying below 10: 2, 5, 8. The stop value is never included, so 11 is not produced.","Objective 2.2 range()",["11 is past the stop value 10; range never produces a number at or beyond its stop.",null,"range starts at its first argument, 2, not at the step value 3.","The step is 3, not 2, so the values go 2, 5, 8 rather than counting by twos."],1],["pc20",0,2,"What does this loop print?\nfor i in range(5, 0, -2):\n    print(i, end=\" \")",["5 3 1 -1","5 4 3 2 1","1 3 5","5 3 1"],3,"A negative step counts down, and the loop stops before reaching the stop value 0: 5, 3, 1.","Objective 2.2 range()",["The loop stops before reaching the stop value 0, so -1 (which is past 0) is never produced.","The step is -2, so every other number is skipped; this output uses a step of -1.","A negative step counts down from the start value 5, so the order is 5, 3, 1, not ascending.",null],2],["pc21",0,2,"What does this code print?\ni = 0\nwhile i < 3:\n    i += 1\nelse:\n    print(\"done\", i)",["done 2","done 3","Nothing","done 4"],1,"The else clause of a while loop runs when the condition becomes false without a break. The loop stops when i reaches 3, then the else prints done 3.","Objective 2.2 while-else",["i is incremented to 3 before the condition i < 3 becomes false, so the else sees 3, not 2.",null,"A while loop's else runs when the condition becomes false without a break, so it does print.","The loop stops as soon as i reaches 3; it never increments i a fourth time."],2],["pc22",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor n in range(5):\n    if n == 3:\n        break\nelse:\n    print(\"no break\")\nprint(n)",["no break, then 3","4","no break, then 4","3"],3,"break leaves the loop when n is 3, and a for loop's else clause is skipped when the loop ends with break. n keeps its last value, 3.","Objective 2.2 for-else and break",["The loop ends with break, so the for loop's else clause is skipped and no break is never printed.","The loop exits at n == 3 via break, so n never reaches its last range value 4.","break skips the else clause, and n stays at 3 rather than reaching 4.",null],3],["pc23",0,2,"A developer wants the sum of the odd numbers from 1 to 5. What does this code print?\ntotal = 0\nfor n in range(1, 6):\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        continue\n    total += n\nprint(total)",["6","15","9","4"],2,"continue skips the rest of the body for even n, so only 1 + 3 + 5 = 9 is added. 6 would be the even numbers and 15 all of them.","Objective 2.2 continue",["6 is the sum of the even numbers 2 and 4; continue skips the evens, so they are the ones not added.","15 is the sum of all numbers 1 to 5; the continue prevents the even numbers from being added.",null,"4 does not correspond to any subset the loop adds; the odd values 1, 3 and 5 are all added."],2],["pc24",0,2,"How many times does the inner loop body run?\nc = 0\nfor i in range(3):\n    for j in range(i):\n        c += 1\nprint(c)",["9","6","3","2"],2,"The inner range(i) runs 0, 1 and 2 times as i goes 0, 1, 2, for a total of 3. 9 assumes the inner loop always runs three times.","Objective 2.2 Nested loops",["9 assumes the inner loop always runs three times; range(i) runs 0, 1 and 2 times as i changes.","6 would be the result of range(i + 1) or summing 1 + 2 + 3; the inner loop runs 0 + 1 + 2 times.",null,"2 counts only the final outer pass; the inner loop also runs once when i is 1."],2],["pc25",0,2,"What does this code print?\nn = 10\nwhile True:\n    n -= 3\n    if n < 0:\n        break\nprint(n)",["-2","1","-1","0"],0,"n goes 10, 7, 4, 1, -2. The check happens after subtracting, so the loop breaks only once n is already -2.","Objective 2.2 break",[null,"1 is the value just before the last subtraction; the loop subtracts 3 again before testing n < 0.","Subtracting 3 repeatedly from 10 gives 7, 4, 1, -2; -1 is never reached.","10 is not a multiple of 3, so subtracting 3 never lands on 0."],1],["pc26",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(0):\n    print(\"x\")\nelse:\n    print(\"empty\")",["x","Nothing at all","empty","x, then empty"],2,"range(0) is empty, so the body never runs. The loop still ends without break, so the else clause prints empty.","Objective 2.2 for-else",["range(0) produces no values, so the loop body that prints x never runs.","The else clause still runs because the loop ended without break, even though it had no iterations.",null,"The body never executes for an empty range, so x is not printed before empty."],2],["pc27",0,2,"A developer writes an if block at the top level of a module (not inside any loop) but has not written its body yet. What should go in the body so the file still runs?",["A comment such as # TODO","pass","continue","break"],1,"pass is a do-nothing statement that satisfies the need for an indented body. A comment alone is not a statement, and continue or break outside a loop are syntax errors.","Objective 2.2 pass",["A comment is ignored by the interpreter and does not count as a statement, so the block would still be empty (IndentationError).",null,"continue is only valid inside a loop; at module level it is a SyntaxError.","break is only valid inside a loop; using it in a top-level if block is a SyntaxError."],3],["pc28",0,2,"A grading script gives every score of 85 a D. What is the bug?\nscore = 85\nif score >= 60:\n    grade = \"D\"\nelif score >= 80:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"F\"\nprint(grade)",["elif needs its own else","85 is compared as a string","The broader condition is tested first, so the elif never runs","The else branch runs first"],2,"Conditions are tested top to bottom and 85 >= 60 matches first, so grade is D and the elif is never reached. The more specific test (>= 80) must come first.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements",["An elif does not require an else; a chain can end with elif or with else, and here it already has an else.","score is assigned the int 85, not a string, so the comparison is numeric.",null,"else is tested last and runs only if every condition above it is false; branches are checked top to bottom."],2],["pc29",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = 0\nwhile x < 10:\n    x += 4\nprint(x)",["10","8","12","9"],2,"x goes 4, 8, 12. The condition is checked only at the start of each pass, so x can overshoot 10 before the loop stops.","Objective 2.2 while",["x increases by 4 each pass (4, 8, 12), so it never equals exactly 10.","8 is still less than 10, so the loop runs one more pass and adds 4 again.",null,"Adding 4 from 0 only produces multiples of 4, so 9 is impossible."],3],["pc30",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor ch in \"abc\":\n    print(ch * 2, end=\"\")",["abcabc","a a b b c c","abc","aabbcc"],3,"A for loop iterates over a string one character at a time; each character is repeated twice and end='' keeps them on one line.","Objective 2.2 Iterating sequences",["ch * 2 repeats each character, not the whole string; the loop handles one character at a time.","end=\"\" prints nothing between items and ch * 2 adds no space, so there are no spaces.","Each character is multiplied by 2, so every letter appears twice, not once.",null],1],["pc31",0,2,"What does this code print?\na = 5\nb = 10\nif a > 3:\n    if b < 5:\n        print(\"X\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Y\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Z\")",["Y","X","Z","Y, then Z"],0,"a > 3 is true, so the outer if runs. Inside it, b < 5 is false, so the nested else prints Y. The outer else is skipped.","Objective 2.1 Nested conditionals",[null,"X prints only if b < 5, but b is 10, so the nested else runs instead.","Z belongs to the outer else, which is skipped because a > 3 is true.","Only one branch of each if-else runs, and the outer else is skipped, so Z is never printed."],2],["pc32",0,2,"How many lines does this code print?\nfor i in range(1, 10, 4):\n    print(\"hi\")",["2","4","3","9"],2,"range(1, 10, 4) produces 1, 5 and 9, so hi prints three times. 9 is included because it is below the stop value 10.","Objective 2.2 range()",["2 misses the value 9, which is below the stop value 10 and is therefore included.","range(1, 10, 4) gives 1, 5 and 9; a fourth value, 13, would exceed the stop.",null,"9 counts every number from 1 to 9, ignoring the step of 4."],2],["pc33",0,2,"What does this code print?\ni = 5\nwhile i > 0:\n    if i == 2:\n        break\n    i -= 1\nelse:\n    print(\"else\")\nprint(i)",["else, then 2","0","else, then 0","2"],3,"i drops from 5 to 2, then break exits the loop. Because the loop ended with break, the while loop's else clause is skipped.","Objective 2.2 while-else and break",["The loop exits with break, so the while loop's else clause is skipped and else is not printed.","break stops the loop at i == 2, so i never counts all the way down to 0.","The loop never reaches 0 and ends with break, so neither else nor 0 is printed.",null],3],["pc34",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(3):\n    pass\nprint(i)",["3","2","0","A NameError"],1,"The loop variable keeps its last value after a for loop ends. range(3) ends at 2, so i is 2. pass does nothing.","Objective 2.2 for",["range(3) produces 0, 1, 2; the loop variable keeps the last value produced, not the stop value 3.",null,"The loop variable is updated on every pass, so it ends at the last value 2, not the first.","A for loop's variable stays defined after the loop ends, so print(i) works."],2],["pc35",0,2,"A student's program prints 0 over and over and never stops. What is the problem?\ncount = 0\nwhile count < 5:\n    print(count)",["print() cannot be used inside a while loop","while needs an else clause to stop","count is never changed inside the loop","The condition must use <= instead of <"],2,"The condition count < 5 stays true forever because nothing in the body changes count. Adding count += 1 fixes it; the comparison operator is not the issue.","Objective 2.2 while",["print() works inside any block, including while loops.","An else clause runs after a loop ends; it cannot make a loop stop.",null,"Changing < to <= would make the loop run even longer; the real problem is that count never changes."],2],["pc36",0,2,"A program must keep asking the user for a PIN until a valid one is entered. How many attempts are needed is not known in advance. Which loop fits best?",["A while loop that repeats until the input is valid","A for loop over range(3)","A single if-else statement","A for loop over the PIN string"],0,"while repeats as long as a condition holds, which suits an unknown number of repetitions. A for loop over range(3) caps the attempts at three.","Objective 2.2 while",[null,"range(3) caps the attempts at exactly three, but the number of attempts is unknown in advance.","A single if-else checks once and cannot repeat the prompt.","Looping over the PIN string visits its characters; it does not repeat the prompt until valid input arrives."],2],["pc37",0,2,"How many times does this code call print()?\nc = 0\nfor i in range(10, 0):\n    c += 1\nprint(c)",["10","1","0","9"],2,"With the default step of +1, range(10, 0) is empty because the start is already past the stop. Counting down needs a negative step.","Objective 2.2 range()",["range(10, 0) with the default step +1 is empty; counting down from 10 needs a negative step.","print(c) is outside the loop, but the loop body never runs, so the printed count is 0, not 1.",null,"No values are produced because the start 10 is already past the stop 0 for a positive step."],2],["pc38",0,2,"How many lines does this nested loop print?\nfor i in range(3):\n    for j in range(3):\n        if j == 1:\n            break\n        print(i, j)",["1","3","9","6"],1,"break exits only the inner loop. For each of the three outer values, the inner loop prints j = 0, then breaks at j = 1: three lines.","Objective 2.2 Nested loops and break",["break exits only the inner loop, so the outer loop still runs all three passes.",null,"9 assumes the inner loop runs fully; break stops it at j == 1 every time.","6 assumes two values print per pass; break happens before print when j is 1, so only j = 0 prints."],3],["pc39",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = 4\nif x > 2 and x < 4:\n    print(\"in\")\nelif x == 4 or x == 5:\n    print(\"edge\")\nelse:\n    print(\"out\")",["edge","in","out","in, then edge"],0,"x > 2 and x < 4 is False because 4 < 4 is False. The elif checks x == 4 or x == 5, which is True, so edge prints.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements",[null,"x < 4 is False when x is 4, so the and expression is False and in is not printed.","else runs only if every earlier condition is false, but x == 4 makes the elif true.","At most one branch of an if-elif-else chain runs, and the first condition is False anyway."],2],["pc40",0,2,"What does this code print?\ntotal = 0\nfor i in range(0, 11, 2):\n    total += i\nprint(total)",["20","25","30","55"],2,"range(0, 11, 2) produces 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10, which add up to 30. 20 forgets that 10 is included because 11 is the stop value.","Objective 2.2 range()",["20 forgets that 10 is included; the stop is 11, so 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10 are all added.","25 is the sum of the odd numbers 1 to 9; this range starts at 0 and steps by 2 through the evens.",null,"55 is the sum of all numbers 0 to 10; the step of 2 skips the odd ones."],2],["pc41",0,2,"A search loop should print Not found only when it checks every item without finding a match. Which construct does this most directly?",["A for loop followed by pass","A for loop with an else clause","A while True loop with continue","An if statement placed before the loop"],1,"The else clause of a for loop runs only when the loop was not ended by break, so breaking on a match skips the Not found message automatically.","Objective 2.2 for-else",["pass does nothing, so placing it after a loop cannot detect whether a match was found.",null,"continue only skips to the next iteration; on its own it gives no signal that the search found nothing.","An if before the loop runs before any items are checked, so it cannot know the search result."],3],["pc42",0,2,"What does this code print?\nn = 3\nwhile n:\n    print(n, end=\" \")\n    n -= 1",["3 2 1 0","2 1 0","3 2 1","Nothing, the loop never starts"],2,"A non-zero int is truthy, so the loop runs while n is 3, 2 and 1, and stops when n becomes 0, which is falsy.","Objective 2.2 while",["The loop stops when n becomes 0 because 0 is falsy, so 0 is never printed.","n is printed before it is decremented, so the first value shown is 3.",null,"A non-zero int such as 3 is truthy, so the loop does start."],2],["pc43",0,2,"What happens when this code is run?\nx = 5\nif x = 5:\n    print(\"yes\")",["It prints yes","It prints nothing","It fails with a SyntaxError","It fails with a NameError"],2,"= is assignment, which is not allowed as an if condition; the comparison operator is ==. The error is caught when the code is parsed, so nothing runs.","Objective 2.1 Conditional statements",["The code never runs; = inside an if condition is a syntax error found during parsing.","The file fails to parse, so it cannot run silently and print nothing.",null,"x is defined, and the problem is detected at parse time as a SyntaxError, not at run time as a NameError."],1],["pc44",0,2,"What does this code print?\ni = 0\nwhile i < 5:\n    i += 1\n    if i == 3:\n        continue\n    print(i, end=\"\")",["1245","12345","12","245"],0,"When i is 3, continue skips the print but the loop keeps going. i is incremented before the check, so 1, 2, 4 and 5 print.","Objective 2.2 continue",[null,"continue skips the print when i is 3, so 3 is not in the output.","continue does not end the loop the way break would; it keeps going after 3.","i is incremented before print, so the first value printed is 1."],1],["pc45",0,3,"What does this code print?\nnums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nprint(nums[1:4], nums[-2])",["[2, 3, 4, 5] 4","[2, 3, 4] 4","[1, 2, 3] 3","[2, 3, 4] 5"],1,"A slice includes the start index but not the stop index, so nums[1:4] is [2, 3, 4]. Index -2 is the second item from the end, 4.","Objective 3.1 Lists: slicing",["The stop index 4 is excluded, so nums[1:4] ends at index 3 and does not include 5.",null,"Indexes start at 0, so nums[1:4] starts at the second item (2), and nums[-2] is 4, not 3.","nums[-2] is the second item from the end (4); -1 would be the last item, 5."],2],["pc46",0,3,"A developer copies a list with b = a and then changes b. What does this print?\na = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\nb.append(4)\nprint(a)",["[1, 2, 3]","[4]","[4, 1, 2, 3]","[1, 2, 3, 4]"],3,"b = a copies the reference, not the list, so both names point to the same list and the append is visible through a. Use a[:] or list(a) for a real copy.","Objective 3.1 Lists: copying",["b = a copies only the reference, so the append through b also changes the list a refers to.","append adds to the existing list; it does not replace the contents with just the new item.","append adds the item at the end, not the front; inserting at the front would need insert(0, 4).",null],2],["pc47",0,3,"What does this code print?\na = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a[:]\nb[0] = 99\nprint(a[0])",["99","1","[99, 2, 3]","An IndexError"],1,"a[:] makes a new list (a shallow copy), so changing b[0] does not affect a. With b = a instead, the answer would be 99.","Objective 3.1 Lists: cloning",["99 would print only if b were an alias of a; a[:] creates a separate copy.",null,"a[0] is a single item, not the whole list, and a was not changed.","Index 0 exists in both lists, so no IndexError occurs."],1],["pc48",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [3, 1, 2]\nlst.insert(1, 9)\nprint(lst)",["[3, 9, 1, 2]","[9, 3, 1, 2]","[3, 1, 9, 2]","[3, 1, 2, 9]"],0,"insert(i, x) puts x before the item currently at index i, so 9 becomes the new item at index 1. append would add it at the end.","Objective 3.1 Lists: methods",[null,"insert(1, 9) places 9 at index 1, not index 0; this output would need insert(0, 9).","9 goes before the item currently at index 1 (the value 1), not after it.","Adding at the end is what append does; insert puts the item at the given index."],2],["pc49",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [5, 3, 8]\nprint(sorted(lst), lst)",["[3, 5, 8] [5, 3, 8]","[3, 5, 8] [3, 5, 8]","None [3, 5, 8]","[5, 3, 8] [3, 5, 8]"],0,"sorted() returns a new sorted list and leaves the original unchanged. The list's own sort() method is the one that sorts in place.","Objective 3.1 Lists: sorted()",[null,"sorted() returns a new list and leaves lst unchanged; only lst.sort() sorts in place.","sorted() returns the sorted list; it is lst.sort() that returns None.","This reverses the roles: sorted() returns the sorted copy and lst stays in its original order."],1],["pc50",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [10, 20, 30, 40]\ndel lst[1:3]\nprint(lst)",["[10, 40]","[10, 30, 40]","[40]","[10, 20, 40]"],0,"del with a slice removes indexes 1 and 2 (20 and 30); the stop index 3 is not included.","Objective 3.1 Lists: del",[null,"The slice 1:3 covers indexes 1 and 2, so both 20 and 30 are removed, not just 20.","Index 0 is outside the slice 1:3, so 10 is kept along with 40.","This removes 30 but keeps 20; the slice starts at index 1, which is 20, so 20 is deleted too."],2],["pc51",0,3,"What does this list comprehension print?\nprint([x * x for x in range(5) if x % 2])",["[0, 4, 16]","[1, 3]","[1, 9, 25]","[1, 9]"],3,"x % 2 is truthy only for odd x, so the filter keeps 1 and 3 from range(5) and squares them. 25 would need x = 5, which range(5) never produces.","Objective 3.1 List comprehensions",["These are the squares of the even numbers; x % 2 is truthy (1) only for odd x.","The comprehension outputs x * x, not x, so the odd values are squared.","25 would need x = 5, but range(5) stops at 4.",null],1],["pc52",0,3,"A program stores a 2 x 3 matrix as nested lists. What does this print?\nm = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\nprint(m[1][0] + m[0][2])",["5","6","7","9"],2,"m[1][0] is row 1, column 0, which is 4, and m[0][2] is row 0, column 2, which is 3. 4 + 3 = 7.","Objective 3.1 Nested lists",["5 is m[1][1] alone; the expression adds m[1][0] (4) and m[0][2] (3).","6 would come from m[1][2] or mixing up rows and columns; m[1][0] is 4 and m[0][2] is 3.",null,"9 would come from adding 6 and 3 (for example using m[1][2]); the first index selects the row, the second the column."],2],["pc53",0,3,"What happens when this code runs?\nt = (1, 2, 3)\nt[0] = 9",["A TypeError is raised","t becomes (9, 2, 3)","An IndexError is raised","The assignment is silently ignored"],0,"Tuples are immutable, so assigning to an item raises TypeError. The index 0 is valid, so it is not an IndexError.","Objective 3.2 Tuples: immutability",[null,"Tuples are immutable, so their items can never be reassigned.","Index 0 is valid for a three-item tuple; the problem is immutability, not the index.","Python does not ignore invalid operations silently; item assignment on a tuple raises an exception."],1],["pc54",0,3,"What does this code print?\nt = (5)\nu = (5,)\nprint(t * 2, u * 2)",["(5, 5) (5, 5)","10 10","(10,) (5, 5)","10 (5, 5)"],3,"Parentheses alone do not make a tuple: (5) is just the int 5, so t * 2 is 10. The trailing comma in (5,) makes a one-item tuple, and * repeats it.","Objective 3.2 Tuples: building",["(5) is just the int 5 in parentheses, so t * 2 is the number 10, not a tuple.","(5,) is a one-item tuple, so u * 2 repeats it to (5, 5) instead of multiplying.","t is an int, so the result is 10 not (10,); multiplying a tuple repeats it, it does not multiply its items.",null],3],["pc55",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\nd[\"c\"] = 3\nd[\"a\"] = 10\nprint(len(d), d[\"a\"])",["3 1","4 10","3 10","2 10"],2,"Assigning to a new key adds it, and assigning to an existing key replaces its value. The dictionary ends with keys a, b and c, and a is 10.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries",["d[\"a\"] = 10 replaces the old value, so d[\"a\"] is 10 afterwards, not 1.","Reassigning an existing key does not add a new entry, so there are 3 keys, not 4.",null,"d[\"c\"] = 3 adds a new key, so the length grows from 2 to 3."],1],["pc56",0,3,"A script reads a setting that is missing from a dictionary. What happens?\nd = {\"x\": 1}\nprint(d[\"y\"])",["A KeyError is raised","None is printed","An IndexError is raised","An empty string is printed"],0,"Square-bracket lookup of a missing key raises KeyError. IndexError is for sequences such as lists; dictionaries do not return None for missing keys with [].","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries",[null,"Square-bracket lookup does not return None for a missing key; d.get(\"y\") would.","IndexError is for sequence positions such as list indexes; dictionaries raise KeyError.","Dictionaries never substitute an empty string for a missing key."],1],["pc57",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\nfor k, v in d.items():\n    print(k, v, end=\";\")",["a;b;","1 a;2 b;","a 1;b 2;","('a', 1);('b', 2);"],2,"items() yields key-value pairs, which the loop unpacks into k and v. Dictionaries keep insertion order, so a comes first.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries: items()",["The loop prints both k and v, so each value appears after its key.","items() yields (key, value) in that order, so the key comes first.",null,"The pair is unpacked into k and v, and print shows them separated by a space, not as a tuple."],2],["pc58",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1}\nprint(1 in d, \"a\" in d)",["True True","True False","False False","False True"],3,"The in operator checks a dictionary's keys, not its values. 1 is a value, so 1 in d is False, while 'a' is a key.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries: key existence",["1 is a value, not a key, and in on a dictionary checks keys only, so 1 in d is False.","This is reversed: \"a\" is a key (True), while 1 is only a value (False).","\"a\" is a key of d, so \"a\" in d is True.",null],1],["pc59",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\ndel d[\"a\"]\nprint(d)",["{'b': 2}","{'a': 1}","{'b': 2, 'a': None}","{}"],0,"del d[key] removes that key and its value from the dictionary, leaving only b.","Objective 3.3 Dictionaries: removing",[null,"del d[\"a\"] removes key a, so it cannot remain in the dictionary.","del removes the key entirely; it does not set its value to None.","Only the named key is deleted; b and its value remain."],2],["pc60",0,3,"A developer tries to capitalize the first letter of a string in place. What happens?\ns = \"hello\"\ns[0] = \"H\"",["s becomes Hello","s becomes H","An IndexError is raised","A TypeError is raised"],3,"Strings are immutable, so item assignment raises TypeError. A new string must be built instead, for example 'H' + s[1:].","Objective 3.4 Strings: immutability",["Strings are immutable, so s[0] = \"H\" cannot change the string in place.","Item assignment is not allowed on strings, and even if it were it would not replace the whole string with H.","Index 0 exists in \"hello\"; the error is about immutability, not a bad index.",null],1],["pc61",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = \"Python\"\nprint(s[::-1], s[1:3])",["nohtyP yth","nohtyP yt","Python yt","nohtyP Py"],1,"A step of -1 walks the string backward, reversing it. s[1:3] takes indexes 1 and 2 (y and t); the stop index is excluded.","Objective 3.4 Strings: slicing",["s[1:3] stops before index 3, so it gives yt, not yth.",null,"s[::-1] with a step of -1 reverses the string rather than returning it unchanged.","s[1:3] starts at index 1 (y), not index 0 (P)."],2],["pc62",0,3,"A CSV line has an empty field. What does this code print?\nprint(\"a,b,,c\".split(\",\"))",["['a', 'b', '', 'c']","['a', 'b', 'c']","['a,b,,c']","['a', 'b', ',', 'c']"],0,"split(',') cuts at every comma, so two commas in a row produce an empty string between them. Empty fields are not dropped when a separator is given.","Objective 3.4 Strings: methods",[null,"With an explicit separator, split() keeps empty fields; only split() with no argument drops empties.","The string does contain the separator, so it is split into several items, not returned whole.","The separator itself is removed and never appears as an item in the result."],1],["pc63",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(\"-\".join([\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"]))",["-x-y-z-","x-y-z","xyz","['x', 'y', 'z']"],1,"sep.join(list) puts the separator only between items and returns one string. No separator is added at the start or end.","Objective 3.4 Strings: methods",["join puts the separator only between items, never at the start or end.",null,"The separator \"-\" is inserted between items; xyz would be \"\".join(...).","join returns a single string, not the list itself."],1],["pc64",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = 'It\\'s'\nprint(len(s))",["5","4","6","A SyntaxError"],1,"\\' is an escaped apostrophe that counts as one character, so the string is It's with length 4. The backslash itself is not stored.","Objective 3.4 Strings: escaping",["The backslash is an escape character and is not stored, so the string has 4 characters, not 5.",null,"Only the four characters I, t, ' and s are stored; neither the backslash nor quotes add length.","\\' is a valid escape sequence inside single quotes, so there is no SyntaxError."],1],["pc65",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(\"abc\" < \"abd\", \"Z\" < \"a\")",["True False","False True","False False","True True"],3,"Strings compare character by character by code point. 'abc' < 'abd' because c < d, and uppercase letters have lower code points than lowercase, so 'Z' < 'a'.","Objective 3.4 Strings: comparison",["\"Z\" < \"a\" is True because uppercase letters have lower code points (Z is 90, a is 97).","\"abc\" < \"abd\" is True because the first differing characters compare c < d.","Both comparisons are True: c comes before d, and Z's code point is below a's.",null],1],["pc66",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = \"\"\"a\nb\"\"\"\nprint(len(s))",["2","4","3","1"],2,"A triple-quoted string keeps the line break as a real newline character, so the string holds a, a newline and b: three characters.","Objective 3.4 Strings: multiline",["The line break inside a triple-quoted string is stored as a newline character, which counts toward the length.","The newline is a single character (\\n), not two, so the length is 3.",null,"The string holds a, a newline and b; len counts all of them, not just lines."],2],["pc67",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [1, 2, 3, 2]\nlst.remove(2)\nprint(lst, lst.index(2))",["[1, 3] 1","[1, 3, 2] 2","[1, 2, 3] 1","[1, 3, 2] 1"],1,"remove() deletes only the first matching value, leaving [1, 3, 2]. index(2) then finds 2 at position 2.","Objective 3.1 Lists: methods",["remove() deletes only the first matching value, so the second 2 stays in the list.",null,"remove(2) does delete a 2, so the list cannot still be [1, 2, 3].","After removal the list is [1, 3, 2], where 2 is at index 2, not index 1."],3],["pc68",0,3,"A program must store the fixed RGB values of a company color so other code cannot change them by accident. Which type fits best?",["A list such as [0, 102, 204]","A tuple such as (0, 102, 204)","A string such as '0,102,204'","A dictionary with index keys"],1,"Tuples are immutable, so their items cannot be reassigned, which protects fixed data. A list works the same way for reading but can be changed.","Objective 3.2 Tuples vs lists",["A list is mutable, so other code could change its items by accident.",null,"A string is immutable but stores text, so the numbers would have to be parsed out each time.","Dictionaries are mutable; their values can be reassigned just like list items."],1],["pc69",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(3 in [1, 2, 3], \"x\" not in \"xyz\")",["True True","False False","False True","True False"],3,"3 is in the list, so the first test is True. in on a string checks for a substring; x is in xyz, so not in gives False.","Objective 3.1 Lists: in and not in",["\"x\" is a substring of \"xyz\", so \"x\" not in \"xyz\" is False.","3 is an element of [1, 2, 3], so the first test is True.","Both results are reversed: 3 is in the list (True), and x is in xyz, so not in is False.",null],2],["pc70",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(a, b=2):\n    return a * b\nprint(f(3), f(3, 4))",["6 6","3 12","A TypeError","6 12"],3,"f(3) uses the default b = 2, giving 6. f(3, 4) overrides the default, giving 12. A default makes the argument optional, not fixed.","Objective 4.2 Default parameters",["f(3, 4) passes 4 for b, which overrides the default, so the second result is 12, not 6.","f(3) uses the default b = 2, so it returns 6, not 3.","Omitting an argument that has a default is allowed, so f(3) raises no TypeError.",null],2],["pc71",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f():\n    print(\"hi\")\nx = f()\nprint(x)",["hi, then None","hi","None","hi, then hi"],0,"f() prints hi when called and has no return statement, so it returns None. That None is stored in x and printed.","Objective 4.1 None and return",[null,"x holds the return value of f(), which is None, and print(x) prints it after hi.","Calling f() runs its body, so hi is printed before None.","hi is printed only once, when f() is called; print(x) prints the return value, None."],2],["pc72",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(a, b, c):\n    return a - b + c\nprint(f(1, c=3, b=2))",["0","2","4","A TypeError"],1,"Keyword arguments match by name, so a = 1, b = 2 and c = 3, giving 1 - 2 + 3 = 2. Keyword arguments can come in any order after positional ones.","Objective 4.2 Keyword arguments",["0 would come from a = 1, b = 3, c = 2; keyword arguments match by name, so b is 2 and c is 3.",null,"4 does not follow from 1 - 2 + 3; it treats the subtraction as addition somewhere.","Mixing one positional argument with keyword arguments in any order is valid, so no TypeError occurs."],3],["pc73",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\ndef f(a, b):\n    return a + b\nprint(f(a=1, 2))",["It prints 3","It prints -1","A NameError is raised","A SyntaxError is raised"],3,"A positional argument cannot follow a keyword argument in a call. Python rejects the code when parsing, before the function ever runs.","Objective 4.2 Mixing argument types",["The call is never executed because a positional argument after a keyword argument is rejected at parse time.","The code cannot run at all, so no subtraction result is possible; the function adds anyway.","The problem is detected by the parser as invalid call syntax, not as an undefined name at run time.",null],2],["pc74",0,4,"A developer writes this function header. What happens?\ndef f(a=1, b):\n    return a + b",["It works, and b defaults to None","A TypeError when the function is called","A SyntaxError, because a default parameter comes before a required one","It works, but only with keyword arguments"],2,"Parameters with default values must come after all parameters without defaults. The definition itself fails with SyntaxError.","Objective 4.2 Default parameters",["Python never gives b an implicit None default; the header itself is invalid.","The error occurs when the def line is parsed, before any call, and it is a SyntaxError, not a TypeError.",null,"The definition is rejected outright, so the function cannot be called with keywords either."],3],["pc75",0,4,"What does this code print?\nx = 10\ndef f():\n    x = 5\n    return x\nprint(f(), x)",["5 5","10 10","10 5","5 10"],3,"Assigning x inside f creates a local variable that shadows the global one. The global x stays 10.","Objective 4.3 Scopes and shadowing",["Assigning x inside f creates a local variable, so the global x stays 10.","f() returns its local x, which is 5, not the global 10.","The order is reversed: f() returns 5 and the global x is 10.",null],2],["pc76",0,4,"What does this code print?\nx = 1\ndef f():\n    global x\n    x += 1\nf()\nprint(x)",["2","1","A NameError","An UnboundLocalError"],0,"The global keyword makes x inside f refer to the module-level x, so x += 1 changes it to 2. Without global the code would raise an error.","Objective 4.3 global keyword",[null,"The global declaration makes x += 1 update the module-level x, so it becomes 2.","x is defined at module level and declared global, so the name is found.","UnboundLocalError happens without global; here global x makes x refer to the module variable."],1],["pc77",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\nx = 1\ndef f():\n    x += 1\n    print(x)\nf()",["It prints 2","It prints 1","An UnboundLocalError is raised","A SyntaxError is raised"],2,"Because x is assigned inside f, Python treats x as local for the whole function. x += 1 then reads the local x before it has a value. Declaring global x would fix it.","Objective 4.3 Scopes",["x += 1 makes x local to f for the whole function, so it cannot read the global 1 and add to it.","The function fails on x += 1 before reaching print, so nothing is printed.",null,"The code is syntactically valid; the problem appears only at run time when the local x is read."],2],["pc78",0,4,"What does this recursive function print?\ndef f(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return n * f(n - 1)\nprint(f(4))",["10","24","12","4"],1,"f(4) = 4 * f(3) = 4 * 3 * f(2) = 4 * 3 * 2 * f(1), and the base case returns 1, so the result is 24 (4 factorial).","Objective 4.1 Recursion",["10 is 4 + 3 + 2 + 1; the function multiplies, it does not add.",null,"12 is 4 * 3 and stops too early; the recursion continues down to f(1).","4 would be returned only if the recursion stopped immediately; f(4) keeps multiplying down to the base case."],2],["pc79",0,4,"A recursive function has no base case, so it always calls itself again. What happens when it runs?\ndef f(n):\n    return f(n + 1)\nf(0)",["It runs forever with no error","It returns None after 10 calls","Python stops it with a RecursionError","A SyntaxError is raised when it is defined"],2,"Python limits recursion depth, so endless recursion raises RecursionError. The definition is valid syntax, so nothing fails until it runs.","Objective 4.1 Recursion",["Python enforces a recursion depth limit, so endless recursion stops with an error rather than running forever.","Nothing limits it to 10 calls, and it never reaches a point where it returns None.",null,"Calling a function from itself is valid syntax, so defining it raises no error."],2],["pc80",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef add(lst):\n    lst.append(1)\nnums = []\nadd(nums)\nadd(nums)\nprint(nums)",["[]","[1]","[1, 1]","None"],2,"A list is passed by reference to the same object, so append inside the function changes the caller's list. Two calls add two items.","Objective 4.2 Passing arguments",["The function receives the same list object, so its appends are visible to the caller.","add() is called twice, and each call appends another 1.",null,"print(nums) prints the list, which is not None; only add()'s return value is None."],2],["pc81",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f():\n    return 1, 2\na, b = f()\nprint(b, a)",["1 2","2 1","(1, 2)","A ValueError"],1,"return 1, 2 returns a tuple, which is unpacked into a and b. The print then shows b first.","Objective 4.1 Returning results",["The print shows b first, so the order is 2 1, not 1 2.",null,"The tuple is unpacked into a and b, so print receives two separate values, not a tuple.","The tuple has exactly two items for two names, so unpacking succeeds without ValueError."],1],["pc82",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\nfor v in gen():\n    print(v, end=\" \")",["1","1 2","2","None"],1,"A function containing yield is a generator. Each yield hands one value to the for loop and resumes afterwards, so both 1 and 2 are printed.","Objective 4.1 yield",["After the first yield the generator resumes and yields 2 as well, so the loop runs twice.",null,"The for loop receives every yielded value in order, starting with 1.","The loop prints the yielded values, not the generator's return value, and yield does not produce None here."],2],["pc83",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    print(1 / 0)\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"zero\")\nexcept ArithmeticError:\n    print(\"arith\")",["arith","zero, then arith","Nothing, the program crashes","zero"],3,"Python runs the first matching except branch only. ZeroDivisionError matches first, so arith is never printed.","Objective 4.5 Ordering except branches",["ZeroDivisionError matches the first branch, so the ArithmeticError branch is never tested.","Only the first matching except branch runs, so arith is not printed after zero.","The exception is caught by the first except branch, so the program does not crash.",null],1],["pc84",0,4,"A developer puts the general branch first. What does this code print?\ntry:\n    print(1 / 0)\nexcept ArithmeticError:\n    print(\"arith\")\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"zero\")",["zero","arith","zero, then arith","Nothing, the program crashes"],1,"ZeroDivisionError is a subclass of ArithmeticError, so the first branch already matches and the more specific branch is never reached. Specific exceptions should come first.","Objective 4.5 Ordering except branches",["The ArithmeticError branch comes first and already matches ZeroDivisionError, so zero is never reached.",null,"Only one except branch runs per exception, so both messages cannot appear.","ArithmeticError catches ZeroDivisionError, so the exception is handled rather than crashing the program."],3],["pc85",0,4,"A program converts user input to a number. What does this code print?\ntry:\n    int(\"abc\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"V\")\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"T\")",["T","V","V, then T","Nothing, the program crashes"],1,"int('abc') has the right type (a string) but a value that cannot be converted, so it raises ValueError. TypeError is for the wrong type, such as int([1]).","Objective 4.4 Built-in exceptions",["int(\"abc\") receives the right type (a string), so it raises ValueError, not TypeError.",null,"Only the first matching except branch runs, and TypeError is never raised.","The ValueError is caught by the first branch, so the program does not crash."],2],["pc86",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    x = [1, 2][5]\nexcept LookupError:\n    print(\"L\")\nexcept IndexError:\n    print(\"I\")",["I","L, then I","Nothing, the program crashes","L"],3,"An out-of-range index raises IndexError, which is a subclass of LookupError, so the first branch catches it.","Objective 4.4 Exception hierarchy",["IndexError is a subclass of LookupError, so the earlier LookupError branch catches it first.","Only one except branch runs, and the first one matches.","The IndexError is caught by the LookupError branch, so nothing crashes.",null],3],["pc87",0,4,"A program uses except Exception: to catch all errors. Which exception will that branch not catch?",["ValueError","KeyboardInterrupt","KeyError","ZeroDivisionError"],1,"KeyboardInterrupt (like SystemExit) inherits directly from BaseException, not from Exception, so the user can still stop the program. The other three are all Exception subclasses.","Objective 4.4 Exception hierarchy",["ValueError is a subclass of Exception, so except Exception catches it.",null,"KeyError inherits from LookupError, which inherits from Exception, so it is caught.","ZeroDivisionError inherits from ArithmeticError and Exception, so it is caught."],3],["pc88",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f():\n    return 1 / 0\ndef g():\n    try:\n        f()\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        print(\"caught in g\")\ng()",["Nothing, the program crashes","caught in g, then None","caught in g","None"],2,"The exception raised in f is not handled there, so it propagates up to the caller g, whose except branch handles it.","Objective 4.5 Propagating exceptions",["The exception propagates from f to g, where the except branch handles it, so the program does not crash.","g() is called as a statement and its return value is not printed, so None does not appear.",null,"The except branch in g prints caught in g, and g's return value is never printed."],2],["pc89",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    print(\"a\" + 1)\nexcept:\n    print(\"E\")",["a1","E","Nothing, the program crashes","E, then a1"],1,"Adding a string and an int raises TypeError. A bare except catches any exception, so E is printed. It is usually better to name the exception.","Objective 4.5 try-except",["\"a\" + 1 raises TypeError instead of converting 1 to text; str(1) would be needed for a1.",null,"A bare except catches every exception, so the TypeError is handled.","The print inside try never completes, so a1 is never produced."],1],["pc90",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(x):\n    if x > 0:\n        return \"pos\"\nprint(f(-1))",["None","pos","0","An error, because there is no return value"],0,"For x = -1 the if body never runs, so the function reaches its end without return and returns None, which print shows.","Objective 4.1 None",[null,"Returning \"pos\" requires x > 0, but -1 is negative, so that return never runs.","A function that ends without return gives back None, not 0.","Reaching the end of a function without return is allowed; it silently returns None."],2],["pc91",0,4,"A function is defined as def area(w, h): and called as area(3, 4). What are 3 and 4?",["Parameters","Arguments","Keywords","Literals of the function"],1,"Parameters are the names in the definition (w and h); arguments are the values passed in the call. They are easy to mix up.","Objective 4.2 Parameters vs arguments",["Parameters are the names in the definition (w and h), not the values passed in the call.",null,"Keywords are reserved words such as def and if; keyword arguments would be written w=3.","3 and 4 are literals in the call, but the term for values passed to a function is arguments."],2],["pc92",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\nprint(sq(3))\ndef sq(n):\n    return n * n",["A NameError is raised","It prints 9","A SyntaxError is raised","It prints None"],0,"Python runs top to bottom, so sq does not exist yet when it is called. Define a function before the line that calls it.","Objective 4.1 Defining and invoking functions",[null,"The def runs after the call, so sq does not exist yet when print(sq(3)) executes.","The code is syntactically valid; the error happens at run time when the undefined name is used.","The call fails before sq can return anything, so None is not printed."],1],["pc93",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    {}[\"k\"]\nexcept (KeyError, IndexError):\n    print(\"lookup\")",["lookup","Nothing, the program crashes","{}","k"],0,"One except branch can name several exceptions in a tuple. Looking up a missing key raises KeyError, which the tuple matches.","Objective 4.5 try-except",[null,"The KeyError matches one of the names in the except tuple, so it is handled.","The expression's result is never printed; the except branch prints lookup.","The missing key name is not printed; only the except branch's message appears."],2],["pc94",0,4,"A script calls exit() to stop early. Which exception does this raise?",["SystemExit","KeyboardInterrupt","StopIteration","RuntimeError"],0,"exit() and sys.exit() raise SystemExit, which inherits from BaseException. KeyboardInterrupt is raised when the user presses Ctrl+C.","Objective 4.4 Exception hierarchy",[null,"KeyboardInterrupt is raised when the user presses Ctrl+C, not by exit().","StopIteration signals that an iterator has no more items; it is unrelated to exiting.","exit() raises SystemExit, a BaseException subclass, not the general RuntimeError."],1],["pc95",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(n, s=\"*\"):\n    return s * n\nprint(f(3), f(2, \"ab\"))",["*** ***","3* 2ab","A TypeError","*** abab"],3,"f(3) uses the default s = '*' and repeats it 3 times. f(2, 'ab') overrides the default, giving abab.","Objective 4.2 Default parameters",["f(2, \"ab\") overrides the default, so the second result is abab, not ***.","s * n repeats the string n times; it does not join the number and the string.","Omitting s is allowed because it has a default, and str * int is valid repetition.",null],1],["pc96",0,4,"What does this code print?\nx = 3\ndef f():\n    return x * 2\nx = 5\nprint(f())",["10","6","A NameError","8"],0,"f only reads x, so it uses the global x as it is when f is called, not when f is defined. x is 5 by then, so f returns 10.","Objective 4.3 Scopes",[null,"f reads x when it is called, not when it is defined; by then x is 5.","Reading a global inside a function needs no declaration, so no NameError occurs.","The function doubles x (5 * 2); it does not add 3 and 5."],3],["pc97",0,4,"A team splits a 300-line script into small functions such as read_scores() and print_report(). What is the main benefit?",["Code is easier to test, reuse and read","The script always runs faster","Variables become global automatically","Exceptions can no longer occur"],0,"Decomposition breaks a problem into named, reusable pieces that can be tested one at a time. It does not make code faster or stop errors.","Objective 4.1 Decomposition",[null,"Splitting code into functions adds small call overhead and does not guarantee faster execution.","Variables assigned inside functions are local by default; decomposition does not make them global.","Functions do not prevent exceptions; errors can still occur and propagate through them."],1],["x1",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(-2 ** 2)",["4","0.25","-4","A SyntaxError"],2,"** binds more tightly than unary minus, so this is -(2 ** 2), which is -4. Getting 4 assumes the minus sign is applied first, as in (-2) ** 2.","Domain 1: Operator priority",["4 would need (-2) ** 2; without parentheses ** is applied before the unary minus.","0.25 would be 2 ** -2; here the minus sign applies to the result, not the exponent.",null,"Unary minus before a power is valid syntax, so no SyntaxError is raised."],2],["x2",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(int(7.9), int(-7.9))",["7 -7","8 -8","7 -8","8 -7"],0,"int() on a float truncates toward zero, dropping the fraction, so 7.9 gives 7 and -7.9 gives -7. The tempting 7 -8 would come from rounding down (floor), which int() does not do.","Domain 1: Type casting",[null,"int() does not round; 7.9 becomes 7, not 8, and -7.9 becomes -7.","-8 would come from floor rounding, but int() truncates toward zero for negative floats too.","Neither value rounds up; int() simply drops the fractional part of each number."],2],["x3",0,1,"A program runs without any error message but prints the wrong average because it divides by the wrong count. In PCEP terms, what kind of error is this?",["A lexical error","A syntax error","An indentation error","A semantic (logic) error"],3,"The code is valid Python and runs, but its meaning is wrong, which is a semantic or logic error. A syntax error would stop the program before it ran at all.","Domain 1: Lexis, syntax and semantics",["A lexical error involves invalid tokens, which would stop the program before it produced any output.","A syntax error prevents the program from running at all, but this one runs and prints a value.","An indentation error is a kind of syntax error and would also stop the program from running.",null],1],["x4",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(\"ab\" * 3 + \"c\")",["ab3c","abababc","ababab c","A TypeError"],1,"* repeats a string, so \"ab\" * 3 is \"ababab\", and + joins \"c\" with no space. A TypeError would occur only when adding a string and a number.","Domain 1: String operators",["* with a string and an int repeats the string; it does not paste the number 3 into the text.",null,"+ joins strings directly and adds no space between \"ababab\" and \"c\".","String * int and string + string are both valid operations, so no TypeError is raised."],1],["x5",0,1,"What does this code print?\nx = 10\nx //= 3\nx **= 2\nprint(x)",["3","9","11.1","9.0"],1,"10 // 3 is the integer 3, and 3 ** 2 is 9. Floor division of two ints gives an int, so the result is 9, not 9.0.","Domain 1: Compound assignment operators",["3 is the value after //= only; the next line squares it with **= 2.",null,"11.1 would need true division and a different operation; //= gives an integer result.","Both operands stay integers after //, so ** 2 produces the int 9, not a float."],2],["x6",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(float(\"1e2\") + int(\"3\"))",["1e23","103","A ValueError","103.0"],3,"float() accepts scientific notation, so \"1e2\" becomes 100.0; adding the int 3 gives the float 103.0. The result is a float, not the int 103, because one operand is a float.","Domain 1: Scientific notation and casting",["float() converts the text to a number, so the strings are not concatenated into 1e23.","One operand is a float (100.0), so the sum is a float and prints with .0.","float() accepts scientific notation such as \"1e2\", so no ValueError is raised.",null],2],["x7",0,1,"A user types 20 and presses Enter. What happens?\nage = input(\"Age: \")\nprint(age + 1)",["A TypeError is raised","21 is printed","201 is printed","20 1 is printed"],0,"input() always returns a string, and adding a str and an int raises a TypeError. Printing 21 would require converting with int(age) first.","Domain 1: Console input",[null,"21 would require int(age); input() returns the string \"20\", which cannot be added to 1.","201 would require str(1) to join strings; adding a str and an int raises an error.","+ does not insert a space, and a str plus an int fails before anything is printed."],2],["x8",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(3 == 3.0, 10 / 5 == 2)",["False True","True False","True True","False False"],2,"Python compares int and float by value, so 3 == 3.0 is True, and 10 / 5 is 2.0, which equals 2. Different types do not make equal numbers compare unequal.","Domain 1: Relational operators",["Python compares numbers by value, so 3 and 3.0 are equal despite different types.","10 / 5 gives 2.0, which compares equal to 2, so the second value is True.",null,"Both comparisons are between equal numeric values, so neither is False."],2],["x9",0,1,"What does this code print?\nCount = 1\ncount = 2\nprint(Count + count)",["4","2","3","A NameError"],2,"Python names are case-sensitive, so Count and count are two different variables holding 1 and 2. Printing 4 assumes the second line overwrote the first variable.","Domain 1: Variables and naming rules",["4 would mean both names refer to the value 2, but Count and count are different variables.","2 would mean Count was replaced, but Python names are case-sensitive.",null,"Both names are defined before use, so no NameError occurs."],1],["x10",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(1 + 2 * 3 > 6 and not False)",["True","False","7","A TypeError"],0,"Arithmetic is evaluated first (7), then the comparison (7 > 6 is True), then not False is True, and True and True is True. The expression never prints 7 because the comparison turns it into a Boolean.","Domain 1: Operator priority",[null,"7 > 6 is True and not False is True, so the and expression is True, not False.","The comparison turns 7 into a Boolean before printing, so 7 is never the result.","Comparing ints and combining Booleans are valid, so no TypeError is raised."],2],["x11",0,1,"What does this code print?\nprint(5 ^ 1, 8 >> 2)",["6 16","4 16","6 2","4 2"],3,"5 is 101 in binary, and XOR with 001 gives 100, which is 4; 8 >> 2 shifts right two places, dividing by 4 to give 2. Reading ^ as addition or >> as a left shift leads to the wrong pairs.","Domain 1: Bitwise operators",["6 would be 5 + 1 and 16 would be a left shift; ^ is XOR and >> shifts right.","4 is right for XOR, but 8 >> 2 shifts right to 2; 16 would need a left shift (8 << 1).","5 ^ 1 is 4 because XOR clears the matching low bit; it is not addition.",null],3],["x12",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = \"\"\nif x:\n    print(\"A\")\nelif not x:\n    print(\"B\")\nelse:\n    print(\"C\")",["A","B","C","Nothing"],1,"An empty string is falsy, so if x fails and not x is True, printing B. The else branch runs only when every earlier condition is false.","Domain 2: Truthy and falsy values",["An empty string is falsy, so the if x branch is skipped.",null,"not x is True for an empty string, so the elif runs before else is reached.","One branch of an if-elif-else chain always runs here because not x is True."],1],["x13",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(1, 10):\n    if i * i > 20:\n        break\nprint(i)",["4","5","9","25"],1,"The loop stops at the first i whose square exceeds 20: 5 * 5 = 25. After break, i keeps its value 5, not the last value in the range.","Domain 2: The loop variable after a loop",["4 * 4 is 16, which is not greater than 20, so the loop keeps going past 4.",null,"break stops the loop at 5, so i never reaches the end of the range.","25 is i * i, but the code prints i itself, not its square."],2],["x14",0,2,"What does this code print?\ncount = 0\nfor i in range(3):\n    while True:\n        count += 1\n        break\nprint(count)",["1","It never stops","0","3"],3,"break exits only the innermost loop, the while True, after one pass, so each of the three for iterations adds 1. It does not loop forever because the break runs every time.","Domain 2: break in nested loops",["break runs on every pass of the for loop, not just the first, so count increases three times.","The break ends the while True loop immediately each time, so it never runs forever.","count += 1 runs before break on every pass, so count cannot stay at 0.",null],2],["x15",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor n in [2, 4, 6]:\n    if n % 2:\n        break\nelse:\n    print(\"all even\")",["all even","Nothing","2 4 6","all even, three times"],0,"Each n % 2 is 0, which is falsy, so break never runs and the else clause runs once after the loop. The else belongs to the for loop, not the if, so it does not run per item.","Domain 2: for-else",[null,"No break runs, so the loop's else clause does run and prints its message.","The loop body never prints n; only the else clause prints anything.","The else belongs to the for loop and runs once after it, not once per item."],2],["x16",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(10, 0, -3):\n    print(i, end=\" \")",["10 7 4","9 6 3","10 7 4 1","10 7 4 1 -2"],2,"The range starts at 10 and steps by -3 while the value stays above the stop value 0: 10, 7, 4, 1. The stop value is excluded, so -2 is never produced.","Domain 2: range() with a negative step",["10 7 4 stops too early; 1 is still greater than the stop value 0, so it is included.","range starts at the start value 10, not 9.",null,"-2 is past the stop value 0, and the stop is never reached or crossed."],2],["x17",0,2,"What does this code print?\nprint(0 or \"x\", 5 and 0)",["False 0","True False","x 0","0 5"],2,"or returns the first truthy operand (or the last one), giving \"x\"; and returns the first falsy operand (or the last one), giving 0. They return operands, not forced True or False values.","Domain 2: Truthy values with and/or",["or returns an operand, not False; 0 is falsy so or moves on to return \"x\".","and and or return one of their operands, not forced True or False values.",null,"or skips the falsy 0 and returns \"x\", and and returns the falsy 0, not 5."],3],["x18",0,2,"What does this code print?\nn = 0\nwhile n > 0:\n    n -= 1\nelse:\n    print(\"else\", n)",["else 0","Nothing","else -1","An infinite loop"],0,"The condition is false at the start, so the body never runs, but the else clause still runs because no break occurred. n is unchanged at 0.","Domain 2: while-else",[null,"The loop's else runs whenever the loop ends without break, even if the body never ran.","The body never runs, so n is never decremented to -1.","The condition is false at the start, so the loop does not run at all, let alone forever."],2],["x19",0,2,"What does this code print?\nc = 0\nfor i in range(4):\n    for j in range(4):\n        if j == i:\n            continue\n        c += 1\nprint(c)",["16","4","8","12"],3,"There are 16 (i, j) pairs, and continue skips the 4 where j equals i, leaving 12. Continue skips only the rest of one inner iteration, not the whole inner loop.","Domain 2: Nested loops and continue",["16 counts every pair, but continue skips the four pairs where j equals i.","4 counts only the skipped pairs, not the pairs that reach c += 1.","8 would mean half the pairs were skipped, but only four of sixteen are.",null],2],["x20",0,2,"Which call produces the values 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 in that order?",["range(5, 1, -1)","range(5, 0, -1)","range(5, 0)","range(1, 5, -1)"],1,"The stop value is excluded, so counting down to 1 needs stop 0 with step -1. range(5, 1, -1) stops before 1 and produces only 5, 4, 3, 2.","Domain 2: range() with a negative step",["range(5, 1, -1) stops before 1, producing only 5, 4, 3, 2.",null,"Without a negative step, range(5, 0) counts upward and is empty.","Starting at 1 with a negative step toward 5 produces an empty range."],1],["x21",0,2,"What does this code print?\na = True\nb = False\nif not a or b:\n    print(\"X\")\nelif a and not b:\n    print(\"Y\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Z\")",["X","Y","Z","X, then Y"],1,"not a or b is False or False, so the first branch is skipped; a and not b is True and True, so Y prints. Only one branch of an if-elif-else chain can run.","Domain 2: Multiple conditions",["not a or b evaluates to False or False, which is False, so X is skipped.",null,"Z runs only if every earlier condition fails, but the elif is True.","Only one branch of an if-elif-else chain runs, so X and Y cannot both print."],2],["x22",0,2,"What does this code print?\nn = 1234\nd = 0\nwhile n > 0:\n    n //= 10\n    d += 1\nprint(d)",["3","1234","5","4"],3,"Each pass removes one digit with // 10: 123, 12, 1, 0, so the loop runs four times. Counting 3 misses the final pass that turns 1 into 0.","Domain 2: while loops",["3 misses the final pass that turns 1 into 0; the loop runs once per digit.","d counts passes; the code never prints the original number.","The loop stops when n reaches 0 after four passes, so there is no fifth pass.",null],2],["x23",0,2,"What does this code print?\nout = \"\"\nfor ch in \"banana\":\n    if ch == \"a\":\n        continue\n    out += ch\nprint(out)",["bnn","aaa","banana","b"],0,"continue skips each \"a\" and the other letters are added in order, giving bnn. Only break would end the loop early and leave just b.","Domain 2: Iterating over strings with continue",[null,"The a characters are the ones skipped, so they are never added to out.","continue skips each a, so the full word is not rebuilt.","continue does not end the loop; only break would stop after b."],1],["x24",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(2):\n    for j in range(2):\n        break\nelse:\n    print(\"outer else\")",["Nothing","outer else, twice","outer else","A SyntaxError"],2,"The break exits only the inner loop, so the outer loop finishes normally and its else runs once. The else is attached to the outer for loop, so it cannot run twice.","Domain 2: for-else with nested loops",["The inner break does not affect the outer loop, which completes and runs its else.","The else belongs to the outer for loop and runs once when it finishes, not per iteration.",null,"else after a for loop is valid syntax, and break inside the inner loop is valid too."],3],["x25",0,2,"What does this code print?\nfor i in range(3):\n    if i == 1:\n        pass\n    print(i, end=\"\")",["02","0","012","12"],2,"pass does nothing, so every value is printed and the output is 012. Only continue would skip the print for i equal to 1.","Domain 2: The pass instruction",["pass does not skip anything; 1 is printed like the other values.","pass does not stop the loop; all three values are printed.",null,"0 is printed on the first pass; nothing skips it."],2],["x26",0,2,"A countdown is meant to stop at 0. What happens when this code runs?\nn = 3\nwhile n != 0:\n    print(n)\n    n -= 2",["It loops forever, since n skips past 0","It prints 3 and 1, then stops","It prints 3, 1 and -1, then stops","It prints nothing at all"],0,"n goes 3, 1, -1, -3 and never equals 0, so the != condition is always true and the loop never ends. Stopping after 1 would need a condition such as n > 0.","Domain 2: Avoiding infinite loops",[null,"After 1, n becomes -1, which is not 0, so the loop does not stop there.","It does not stop after -1 either, because n keeps decreasing and never equals 0.","n is 3 at the start, which is not 0, so the body runs and prints."],3],["x27",0,2,"How many times does this loop body run?\nfor i in range(-3, 3):\n    print(i)",["3","5","0","6"],3,"range(-3, 3) produces -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, which is six values. A start below zero is valid, so the range is not empty.","Domain 2: for loops over range()",["3 counts only the non-negative values; the range also includes -3, -2 and -1.","5 miscounts; -3 through 2 inclusive is six values because the stop 3 is excluded.","A negative start is valid and smaller than the stop, so the range is not empty.",null],1],["x28",0,2,"What does this code print?\nx = 15\nif x > 10:\n    if x > 20:\n        print(\"big\")\nprint(\"done\")",["big, then done","done","big","Nothing"],1,"The outer condition is true but the inner one is false, so big is skipped; done is not indented under either if, so it always prints.","Domain 2: Nested conditionals and indentation",["big needs x > 20, which is false for 15.",null,"big does not print because the inner condition is false, and done always prints.","done is outside both if blocks, so it prints regardless of the conditions."],1],["x29",0,3,"What does this code print?\nlst = [3, 1, 2]\nresult = lst.sort()\nprint(result)",["[1, 2, 3]","None","[3, 1, 2]","An AttributeError"],1,"sort() sorts the list in place and returns None, so result is None. sorted(lst) is the function that returns a new sorted list.","Domain 3: List methods",["sort() sorts in place and returns None; sorted() returns the new list.",null,"sort() does change lst, but result holds the return value None, not the list.","Lists do have a sort() method, so there is no AttributeError."],2],["x30",0,3,"What does this code print?\nm = [[0] * 2] * 2\nm[0][0] = 5\nprint(m)",["[[5, 0], [0, 0]]","[[5, 5], [0, 0]]","[5, 0, 0, 0]","[[5, 0], [5, 0]]"],3,"Multiplying the outer list copies the reference, so both rows are the same inner list, and changing one changes both. Independent rows would need a comprehension such as [[0] * 2 for _ in range(2)].","Domain 3: Nested lists and aliasing",["Both rows are the same list object, so the change shows in both rows.","Only index 0 of the shared inner list changes, not every item.","The structure stays nested; multiplying the outer list does not flatten it.",null],3],["x31",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = \"abcdef\"\nprint(s[-3:], s[:-3])",["def abc","abc def","fed abc","def cba"],0,"s[-3:] takes the last three characters, and s[:-3] takes everything except the last three. Neither slice reverses the string; that needs a negative step.","Domain 3: String slicing",[null,"s[-3:] is the end of the string and s[:-3] is the start, not the other way round.","These slices have no negative step, so nothing is reversed.","s[:-3] keeps the first three characters in their original order."],2],["x32",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\nprint(\"1\" in d.values(), 2 in d.values())",["True True","True False","False True","False False"],2,"The values are the integers 1 and 2, so the string \"1\" is not among them but the integer 2 is. Python does not treat \"1\" and 1 as equal.","Domain 3: Dictionary values()",["The string \"1\" does not equal the integer 1, so the first check is False.","The first check is False, not True, and the second finds the integer 2.",null,"The integer 2 is one of the values, so the second check is True."],2],["x33",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(len((\"ab\")), len((\"ab\",)))",["1 1","2 2","2 1","1 2"],2,"Parentheses alone do not make a tuple, so (\"ab\") is just the string of length 2; the trailing comma makes (\"ab\",) a one-item tuple. The comma, not the brackets, builds the tuple.","Domain 3: One-item tuples",["(\"ab\") is just a string of length 2, not a one-item tuple.","The trailing comma makes a tuple with one item, so its length is 1.",null,"The lengths are reversed; the string has 2 characters and the tuple has 1 item."],2],["x34",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(\"  Hi There \".strip().lower().replace(\" \", \"_\"))",["hi_there","__hi_there_","hi there","Hi_There"],0,"strip() removes the outer spaces first, lower() makes it lowercase, and replace() swaps the single inner space for an underscore. Because stripping happens first, no extra underscores appear.","Domain 3: String methods",[null,"strip() runs before replace(), so the outer spaces are gone before any underscores are added.","replace() swaps the inner space for an underscore, so a space no longer appears.","lower() runs before printing, so the capital letters do not survive."],2],["x35",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(\"banana\".find(\"n\"), \"banana\".count(\"an\"), \"banana\".find(\"z\"))",["3 2 0","2 3 -1","2 2 0","2 2 -1"],3,"The first n is at index 2, \"an\" occurs twice, and find() returns -1 when nothing is found. It does not return 0, which would be a valid index.","Domain 3: String methods",["The first n is at index 2 (b=0, a=1, n=2), and find() returns -1 when nothing is found.","\"an\" appears twice in banana, not three times.","find() returns -1 for a missing substring, since 0 would be a valid index.",null],2],["x36",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint([c.upper() for c in \"a1b2\" if c not in \"12\"])",["['A', '1', 'B', '2']","['A', 'B']","['a', 'b']","AB"],1,"The condition keeps only characters that are not digits 1 or 2, and upper() converts them, producing a list. The result is a list, not the joined string AB.","Domain 3: List comprehensions with conditions",["The condition drops the digits 1 and 2, so they are not in the result.",null,"upper() is applied to each kept character, so the letters are capitals.","A list comprehension builds a list, not a joined string."],2],["x37",0,3,"What does this code print?\nd = {\"b\": 2, \"a\": 1}\nfor k in sorted(d.keys()):\n    print(k, end=\"\")",["ba","ab","12","a1b2"],1,"sorted() returns the keys in alphabetical order, and only the keys are printed. Printing ba would follow insertion order, which sorted() overrides.","Domain 3: Iterating dictionaries",["ba is insertion order, but sorted() puts the keys in alphabetical order.",null,"The loop prints keys from keys(), not values, so 12 does not appear.","Only k is printed, so the values never appear in the output."],1],["x38",0,3,"What does this code print?\na = [1, 2, 3]\nb = a\na = a + [4]\nprint(b)",["[1, 2, 3, 4]","[4]","None","[1, 2, 3]"],3,"a + [4] builds a new list and rebinds a to it, while b still refers to the original list. Using a.append(4) would have changed the shared list and affected b.","Domain 3: Copying vs aliasing lists",["a + [4] creates a new list for a; b still refers to the original unchanged list.","Nothing reduces b to [4]; it still holds the original three items.","b refers to a list, not the result of a method, so it is not None.",null],3],["x39",0,3,"What does this code print?\nt = (10, 20, 30, 40)\nprint(t[1:3], t[-1])",["(20, 30) 40","[20, 30] 40","(20, 30, 40) 40","(10, 20) 30"],0,"Slicing a tuple returns a tuple of items at indexes 1 and 2, and t[-1] is the last item. A slice of a tuple is a tuple, not a list.","Domain 3: Tuple indexing and slicing",[null,"Slicing a tuple returns a tuple, shown in parentheses, not a list.","The slice stops before index 3, so 40 is not included.","Index 1 is 20, so the slice starts at 20, not 10, and t[-1] is 40."],1],["x40",0,3,"What does this code print?\ncounts = {}\nfor ch in \"abca\":\n    if ch in counts:\n        counts[ch] += 1\n    else:\n        counts[ch] = 1\nprint(counts[\"a\"], len(counts))",["1 4","2 4","2 3","1 3"],2,"The letter a appears twice, and the dictionary holds three distinct keys: a, b and c. len() counts keys, not the four characters processed.","Domain 3: Building dictionaries",["a appears twice, and len() counts distinct keys (3), not characters (4).","len() counts the three distinct keys, not the four characters processed.",null,"counts[\"a\"] is 2 because a appears twice in abca."],2],["x41",0,3,"What does this code print?\ns = \"cat\"\nt = s.upper()\nprint(s, t)",["CAT CAT","cat cat","cat CAT","An error, since strings are immutable"],2,"Strings are immutable, so upper() returns a new string and leaves s unchanged. Calling a method that returns a new string is allowed; only item assignment raises an error.","Domain 3: String immutability",["upper() returns a new string; s itself is unchanged and stays lowercase.","t holds the result of upper(), so it is CAT, not cat.",null,"Calling a method that returns a new string is allowed; only item assignment is an error."],1],["x42",0,3,"What does this code print?\nprint(len(\"a\\tb\\\\\"))",["4","5","6","3"],0,"\\t is one tab character and \\\\ is one backslash, so the string is a, tab, b, backslash: four characters. Counting each escape as two characters gives 6.","Domain 3: Escape sequences",[null,"5 counts one of the escapes as two characters; each escape is one character.","6 counts both escapes as two characters each, but each is a single character.","3 drops a character; the string holds a, tab, b and a backslash."],3],["x43",0,3,"What does this code print?\nm = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nprint(3 in m, [3, 4] in m)",["True True","True False","False False","False True"],3,"in checks only the top-level items of m, which are the two inner lists, so 3 is not found but [3, 4] is. in does not search inside nested lists.","Domain 3: in with nested lists",["in checks only top-level items, so 3 inside a nested list is not found.","The values are reversed: 3 is not a top-level item, but [3, 4] is.","[3, 4] is one of the top-level items, so the second check is True.",null],2],["x44",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef a():\n    return int(\"x\")\ndef b():\n    try:\n        return a()\n    except ValueError:\n        return -1\nprint(b())",["x","-1","None","A ValueError stops the program"],1,"The ValueError raised in a() propagates up to b(), where the except branch catches it and returns -1. The program does not crash because the caller handles it.","Domain 4: Propagating exceptions",["int(\"x\") never succeeds, so the text x is not returned or printed.",null,"The except branch returns -1 explicitly, so the result is not None.","b() catches the ValueError, so it does not reach the top level and stop the program."],2],["x45",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(a, b=2, c=3):\n    return a + b * c\nprint(f(1, c=10))",["13","21","31","A TypeError"],1,"a is 1, b keeps its default 2, and c is 10, so the result is 1 + 2 * 10 = 21. 31 comes from adding before multiplying, which ignores operator priority.","Domain 4: Default and keyword arguments",["13 would use c = 3 and b = 10; the keyword sets c, and b keeps its default 2.",null,"31 adds before multiplying, but * has priority over +.","Keyword arguments after positional ones are valid, so no TypeError is raised."],2],["x46",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(x):\n    if x:\n        return\n    return 0\nprint(f(1), f(0))",["1 0","0 0","None None","None 0"],3,"A bare return returns None, so f(1) is None, while f(0) skips the if and returns 0. A bare return does not hand back the argument.","Domain 4: return and None",["A bare return gives None, not the argument 1.","f(1) takes the bare return and gives None, not 0.","f(0) skips the if and reaches return 0, so it is not None.",null],2],["x47",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\ndef f(a):\n    return a\nf(1, 2)",["A TypeError is raised","It returns 1","It returns (1, 2)","A SyntaxError is raised"],0,"Calling a function with more positional arguments than it has parameters raises a TypeError at call time. The code is syntactically valid, so it is not a SyntaxError.","Domain 4: Positional arguments",[null,"Python does not ignore extra arguments; the call fails before returning.","Extra positional arguments are not packed into a tuple without a *args parameter.","The call is valid syntax; the mismatch is detected only when it runs."],1],["x48",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntry:\n    [1, 2, 3].index(5)\nexcept IndexError:\n    print(\"I\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"V\")\nexcept:\n    print(\"other\")",["I","other","V","Nothing, since index() returns -1"],2,"list.index() raises ValueError when the value is missing, so the ValueError branch runs. IndexError is for a bad position, not a missing value.","Domain 4: Common built-in exceptions",["IndexError is for a bad position; a missing value in index() raises ValueError.","The ValueError branch matches first, so the bare except is never reached.",null,"list.index() raises ValueError; str.find() is the method that returns -1."],3],["x49",0,4,"Which built-in exception is a subclass of LookupError?",["ZeroDivisionError","ValueError","KeyError","TypeError"],2,"KeyError and IndexError both derive from LookupError. ZeroDivisionError derives from ArithmeticError instead.","Domain 4: The exception hierarchy",["ZeroDivisionError derives from ArithmeticError, not LookupError.","ValueError is a direct subclass of Exception, not of LookupError.",null,"TypeError is a direct subclass of Exception, not of LookupError."],1],["x50",0,4,"ZeroDivisionError derives directly from which exception class?",["ArithmeticError","LookupError","ValueError","KeyboardInterrupt"],0,"ZeroDivisionError is a subclass of ArithmeticError, so except ArithmeticError also catches it. ValueError sits directly under Exception and is not its parent.","Domain 4: The exception hierarchy",[null,"LookupError is the parent of KeyError and IndexError, not of ZeroDivisionError.","ValueError sits directly under Exception, alongside ArithmeticError; it is not the parent of ZeroDivisionError.","KeyboardInterrupt derives from BaseException and is unrelated to arithmetic errors."],1],["x51",0,4,"What happens when this code runs, if value was never defined?\ntry:\n    print(value)\nexcept (TypeError, ValueError):\n    print(\"bad value\")",["bad value is printed","None is printed","Nothing is printed and the program continues","The program stops with a NameError"],3,"Using an undefined name raises NameError, which is not in the handled tuple, so it is not caught and the program stops. bad value would print only for TypeError or ValueError.","Domain 4: try-except with several exceptions",["bad value prints only for TypeError or ValueError; an undefined name raises NameError.","An undefined name is an error, not a variable holding None.","The NameError is not in the handled tuple, so the program does not continue.",null],2],["x52",0,4,"What does this recursive function print?\ndef s(n):\n    if n < 10:\n        return n\n    return n % 10 + s(n // 10)\nprint(s(472))",["472","13","4","11"],1,"Each call adds the last digit and recurses on the rest: 2 + 7 + 4 = 13, with the base case returning the single digit 4. Stopping at the base case value alone gives 4.","Domain 4: Recursion and base cases",["472 would be returned only if the base case fired first, but 472 is not below 10.",null,"4 is only the base case value; the other digits are added as calls return.","11 misses one digit; 2 + 7 + 4 is 13."],3],["x53",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f(x):\n    x = x * 2\n    return x\nx = 3\nprint(f(x), x)",["6 6","6 3","3 3","3 6"],1,"The parameter x is local to f, so doubling it does not change the global x, which stays 3. Only a global statement would link the two names.","Domain 4: Name scopes and shadowing",["The global x is not changed, because the parameter x is a separate local name.",null,"f returns the doubled value 6, not the original 3.","The values are reversed; f returns 6 and the global x stays 3."],2],["x54",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef setup():\n    global limit\n    limit = 5\nsetup()\nprint(limit)",["A NameError","None","0","5"],3,"The global statement makes the assignment create limit in the module scope, so it exists after setup() runs. Without global the name would be local and printing it would raise NameError.","Domain 4: The global keyword",["global makes the assignment create limit at module level, so the name exists.","limit is assigned 5, not None.","No code sets limit to 0; it is assigned 5.",null],2],["x55",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef f():\n    for i in range(3):\n        return i\nprint(f())",["0","[0, 1, 2]","2","None"],0,"return ends the function immediately on the first pass, returning 0. Collecting all values would need a list or yield, not return inside the loop.","Domain 4: return",[null,"return exits on the first pass, so the values are never collected into a list.","The loop never reaches i = 2 because return ends the function at i = 0.","The function returns 0 explicitly, so it does not fall through to None."],2],["x56",0,4,"What does this code print?\ndef a():\n    return b() + 1\ndef b():\n    return 1\nprint(a())",["A NameError, since b is defined after a","1","2","None"],2,"Names in a function body are looked up when the function runs, and b is defined before a() is called, so the result is 2. Defining b after a is fine as long as both exist before the call.","Domain 4: Defining and invoking functions",["b is looked up when a() runs, and by then b exists, so no NameError occurs.","a() adds 1 to b()'s result of 1, so it returns 2.",null,"a() returns b() + 1 explicitly, so the result is not None."],3],["x57",0,4,"A helper parse_age(text) returns int(text). Three callers need different reactions to bad input: one re-prompts, one logs and skips, one uses a default. Where should the ValueError be handled?",["Inside parse_age, always returning 0 for bad input","Nowhere, because ValueError cannot be caught","In each caller, since each needs a different reaction","With a bare except around the whole program"],2,"Letting the exception propagate lets each caller choose its own response. Handling it inside the helper forces one reaction on everyone and hides the error with a fake 0.","Domain 4: Deciding where to handle exceptions",["Returning 0 hides bad input and forces one reaction on callers that need different ones.","ValueError is an ordinary exception and can be caught with try-except.",null,"A bare except around everything catches too much, including exits, and removes each caller's choice."],3],["x58",0,4,"Which class is the base of all built-in exceptions, including SystemExit and KeyboardInterrupt?",["BaseException","Exception","ArithmeticError","RuntimeError"],0,"BaseException is the root of the hierarchy; SystemExit and KeyboardInterrupt derive from it directly. Exception sits below BaseException and does not include those two.","Domain 4: The exception hierarchy",[null,"Exception is below BaseException and does not include SystemExit or KeyboardInterrupt.","ArithmeticError is a subclass of Exception for math errors only.","RuntimeError is a subclass of Exception, far from the root of the hierarchy."],1],["x59",0,4,"What happens when this code runs?\ndef f(a, b):\n    return a - b\nprint(f(1, a=2))",["It prints 1, with a=2 and b=1","A SyntaxError is raised","It prints -1, with a=1 and b=2","A TypeError is raised"],3,"The positional 1 already fills a, so a=2 gives a a second value and Python raises TypeError. The call is syntactically legal because the keyword comes after the positional argument.","Domain 4: Mixed argument passing",["Python does not let a keyword override a positional argument; a would get two values.","A keyword after a positional argument is legal syntax; the error happens at call time.","a=2 is not ignored; it clashes with the positional 1 and raises an error.",null],3],["x60",0,4,"What does this code print?\ntotal = 0\nfor s in [\"4\", \"x\", \"6\"]:\n    try:\n        total += int(s)\n    except ValueError:\n        continue\nprint(total)",["4","10","A ValueError","46"],1,"int(\"x\") raises ValueError, which is caught, and continue moves on, so 4 and 6 are added to give 10. The loop does not stop at the bad item because the exception is handled.","Domain 4: try-except in a loop",["The bad item is skipped, but the loop continues and adds 6 as well.",null,"The ValueError is caught by the except branch, so it does not stop the program.","int() converts each string to a number, so the values are added, not joined."],2]]);
